The climate of [[Sinchew]] is {{w|tropical climate|tropical}} and strongly maritime, dominated by the seasonal migration of the {{w|Intertropical Convergence Zone}} across a large peninsula-continent. The country spans roughly 10.3 degrees of latitude, from approximately 0.7°N to 11°S, and occupies approximately 2.02 million km² of western [[Lurandia]]. Annual precipitation ranges from 795 mm in the western interior to 5,719 mm on the highest windward slopes, with a national median of 2,369 mm; mean annual temperature ranges from 5.3 °C on the highest summits to 27.0 °C in the northern lowlands, with a median of 21.9 °C. No part of the country is arid.

Two gradients organise the climate. The first is latitudinal: rainfall and temperature decline steadily southward, producing a transition from {{w|tropical rainforest climate|tropical rainforest}} in the north through {{w|tropical monsoon climate|monsoon}} and {{w|tropical savanna climate|savanna}} belts to {{w|humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical}} conditions in the far south. The second is orographic: a mountain spine running along the eastern coast intercepts the prevailing easterly {{w|trade winds}}, producing some of the highest rainfall totals in the country on its windward slopes and a pronounced {{w|rain shadow}} to the west.

Sinchew is overwhelmingly coastal. Approximately 86.7 per cent of its national boundary meets the sea, with the only substantial land frontiers being with [[Etowah]] to the south, [[Amiera]] to the east, and [[Balboa]] to the northeast. The open [[Shendan Ocean]] lies to the north, while the western and south-western coasts border a semi-enclosed marginal sea separated from [[Louyang]] by open water. This maritime setting moderates temperatures throughout the country and ensures that no part of Sinchew lies far from a moisture source.

== Climate controls ==

The principal controls on Sinchew's climate are its equatorial latitude, its near-total maritime exposure, the seasonal migration of the Intertropical Convergence Zone, and the eastern highlands.

=== Latitude and solar regime ===

Sinchew lies almost entirely within the southern tropics. Mean annual temperatures exceed 25 °C across the northern lowlands and decline to roughly 20 °C in the southern interior, a gradient driven principally by latitude and modified by elevation. Because the country straddles the equator and extends to 11°S, the seasonal cycle is governed less by solar angle than by the position of the convergence zone.

=== Atmospheric circulation ===

The prevailing surface flow is easterly, as {{w|trade winds}} converge on the Intertropical Convergence Zone. Over the course of a year the convergence zone migrates between roughly 12°S in the southern summer and 8°N in the southern winter, traversing the full latitudinal extent of Sinchew. Regions near the equator receive two rainfall maxima annually as the zone passes overhead in each direction, while the southern interior receives a single concentrated wet season.

Because the country lies predominantly in the southern hemisphere, the {{w|southeast trade winds}} dominate for most of the year, drawing moisture from the warm marginal sea to the south and south-east. In the northernmost portion, the {{w|northeast trade winds}} prevail seasonally, drawing on the open ocean.

=== Maritime influence ===

With 86.7 per cent of its boundary meeting water, Sinchew experiences pronounced maritime moderation. Seasonal temperature ranges are muted near all coasts, and humidity remains high year-round. The marginal sea to the west and south-west is shallow and semi-enclosed, and consequently warmer than open ocean at comparable latitudes, supplying substantial evaporation and supporting {{w|tropical cyclone}} formation. The open Shendan Ocean to the north moderates the northern coast.

=== Orographic effects ===

The eastern highlands, a mountain spine running the length of the eastern coast and reaching a maximum elevation of 2,625 m, exert the strongest local control on rainfall distribution. Easterly trade winds are forced to ascend the windward slopes, producing orographic rainfall that makes the eastern highlands the wettest part of the country. Air descending to the west is correspondingly drier, and rainfall declines steadily westward across the interior. The driest parts of Sinchew are accordingly found in the western and south-western interior rather than on any coast.

== Precipitation ==

[[File:Sinchew annual precipitation.png|thumb|300px|Annual precipitation across Sinchew]]
<!-- Image prompt: clean thematic map of Sinchew showing annual precipitation in mm/yr, YlGnBu colour ramp from 0 to 5500 mm, no axis labels, with a vertical colour bar labelled "mm per year" -->

Annual precipitation across Sinchew ranges from approximately 795 mm in the driest western interior to 5,719 mm on the highest windward slopes, with a national median of 2,369 mm. The distribution is governed jointly by proximity to the moisture-bearing eastern margin and by elevation, producing a strong west-to-east gradient superimposed on a general decline from north to south.

The northern third of the country, closest to the equatorial convergence zone and with the lowest elevations, is uniformly wet, with annual totals generally above 4,000 mm. The upper-central latitudes receive between 2,000 and 3,500 mm, with the total depending chiefly on position relative to the eastern highlands. The lower-central and southern interior is the driest portion of the country, with totals between roughly 1,200 and 2,000 mm. Even here, however, no part of Sinchew is arid; the national minimum of 795 mm is comparable to the wetter end of a {{w|Mediterranean climate}}.

Rainfall is strongly seasonal in the south and only weakly so in the north. The southern interior experiences a single wet season concentrated in the southern summer, when the convergence zone lies to the south of the country and the southeast trades deliver moisture inland. Northern regions receive two maxima annually as the convergence zone passes overhead in both directions. The seasonal contrast is sufficient to separate {{w|tropical monsoon climate|monsoon}} from {{w|tropical savanna climate|savanna}} classifications across the central latitudes.

== Temperature ==

[[File:Sinchew mean annual temperature.png|thumb|300px|Mean annual temperature across Sinchew]]
<!-- Image prompt: clean thematic map of Sinchew showing mean annual temperature in degrees Celsius, RdYlBu_r colour ramp from 4 to 28 C, no axis labels, with a vertical colour bar labelled "degrees Celsius" -->

Mean annual temperature across Sinchew ranges from 5.3 °C on the highest summits to 27.0 °C in the northern lowlands, with a national median of 21.9 °C. In the lowlands the range is narrow: the northern plains average 25–26.6 °C, the southern interior 20–21 °C. The principal departures from this latitudinal pattern are elevation-driven, with temperatures falling approximately 6.5 °C per kilometre of ascent, and maritime, with coastal districts exhibiting a reduced annual range relative to the interior at the same latitude.

Frost is unknown in the lowlands at any latitude. It occurs only at the highest elevations, where mean annual temperatures fall below 8 °C and conditions approach {{w|tundra|tundra}}.

== Köppen classification ==

[[File:Sinchew Koppen climate classification.png|thumb|300px|Köppen climate types of Sinchew]]
<!-- Image prompt: categorical thematic map of Sinchew showing Köppen climate classes in distinct flat colours (Af dark blue, Am mid blue, Aw green, Cfa pale green, Cfb teal, ET grey), no axis labels, discrete colour legend listing the class names -->

Under the {{w|Köppen climate classification}}, Sinchew contains six climate types, all of them tropical or subtropical. The classification is dominated by the {{w|tropical rainforest climate|Af}}, {{w|tropical monsoon climate|Am}} and {{w|tropical savanna climate|Aw}} types, which together account for about 81 per cent of the national territory.

