The {{w|tropical rainforest climate}} is confined to the northern lowlands, where rainfall is high in all months. The {{w|tropical monsoon climate}} occupies a broad belt across the upper-central latitudes and is characterised by a short dry season insufficient to suppress forest cover. The {{w|tropical savanna climate}} covers the lower-central and southern interior, where a pronounced dry season limits vegetation to savanna woodland and grassland.

The two subtropical types are spatially restricted. {{w|Humid subtropical climate}} appears in southern lowlands that retain adequate rainfall year-round despite their latitude, while the {{w|subtropical highland climate}} is confined to the eastern highlands and is the only type in the country driven principally by elevation rather than by latitude or maritime position. Small areas of {{w|tundra}} occur on the highest summits above roughly 2,400 m.

